CRYPTOGRAPHY PYTHON — STRING / BYTES / HEX NOTES
===================================================

1. BASIC CONVERSIONS

String -> Bytes:
    text.encode()

Fixed string -> Bytes:
    b"Hello"

Bytes -> String:
    data.decode()

Bytes -> Hex string:
    data.hex()

Hex string -> Bytes:
    bytes.fromhex("48656c6c6f")


2. IMPORTANT TYPES

    "A1B2C3D4"   -> str
    b"A1B2C3D4"  -> bytes

A value such as:
    "48656C6C6F"

is still a STRING containing hexadecimal characters.

Convert it to actual bytes using:
    bytes.fromhex("48656C6C6F")


3. ENCRYPTION AND DECRYPTION

In common Python crypto libraries such as PyCryptodome:

    ciphertext = cipher.encrypt(data)

returns BYTES.

And:

    plaintext = cipher.decrypt(ciphertext)

also returns BYTES.

Therefore:

    encrypt() -> bytes
    decrypt() -> bytes


4. input() ALWAYS RETURNS A STRING

    text = input("Enter text: ")

The result is always a STRING.

If the user enters normal text:
    data = text.encode()

If the user enters hexadecimal:
    data = bytes.fromhex(text)


5. .hex() IS NOT ENCRYPTION

Encryption:
    ciphertext = cipher.encrypt(data)

Displaying ciphertext as hexadecimal:
    print(ciphertext.hex())

.hex() only converts BYTES into a hexadecimal STRING for representation/display.


6. PADDING

Block ciphers require data to fit their block size.

DES block size:
    8 bytes = 64 bits

AES block size:
    16 bytes = 128 bits

Padding is performed on BYTES:

    padded = pad(data, DES.block_size)

After decryption:

    decrypted = cipher.decrypt(ciphertext)
    decrypted = unpad(decrypted, DES.block_size)

Then, if the original data was text:

    text = decrypted.decode()


7. NORMAL-TEXT ENCRYPTION PIPELINE

    USER INPUT
        |
        v
      STRING
        |
        | .encode()
        v
      BYTES
        |
        | pad()
        v
    PADDED BYTES
        |
        | encrypt()
        v
    CIPHERTEXT BYTES
        |
        | .hex()
        v
    HEX STRING FOR DISPLAY


8. HEX-CIPHERTEXT DECRYPTION PIPELINE

    HEX CIPHERTEXT STRING
        |
        | bytes.fromhex()
        v
    CIPHERTEXT BYTES
        |
        | decrypt()
        v
    DECRYPTED BYTES
        |
        | unpad()
        v
    PLAINTEXT BYTES
        |
        | .decode()
        v
    PLAINTEXT STRING


9. SIMPLE EXAMPLE

    text = "Hello"

    a = text.encode()
    # b'Hello'

    b = a.hex()
    # '48656c6c6f'

    c = bytes.fromhex(b)
    # b'Hello'

    d = c.decode()
    # 'Hello'


10. DES IMPORTANT SIZES

DES:
    Block size = 64 bits = 8 bytes
    Key input = 64 bits = 8 bytes
    Effective key strength = 56 bits

DES-CBC:
    IV size = 64 bits = 8 bytes

3DES (3-key):
    Key input = 192 bits = 24 bytes
    Block size = 64 bits = 8 bytes


11. AES IMPORTANT SIZES

AES:
    Block size = 128 bits = 16 bytes

AES-128:
    Key = 128 bits = 16 bytes

AES-192:
    Key = 192 bits = 24 bytes

AES-256:
    Key = 256 bits = 32 bytes


12. COMMON PYTHON CRYPTO PATTERNS

Normal text:
    plaintext = input("Enter plaintext: ")
    plaintext = plaintext.encode()

Hex input:
    hex_data = input("Enter data in HEX: ")
    data = bytes.fromhex(hex_data)

Encryption:
    ciphertext = cipher.encrypt(data)

Display encrypted data as HEX:
    print(ciphertext.hex())

Decryption:
    plaintext = cipher.decrypt(ciphertext)

Convert decrypted bytes to text:
    print(plaintext.decode())


13. DO NOT MIX THESE UP

NORMAL TEXT:
    "Hello"
        |
        | .encode()
        v
    b"Hello"

HEX TEXT:
    "48656c6c6f"
        |
        | bytes.fromhex()
        v
    b"Hello"

BYTES:
    b"Hello"
        |
        | .hex()
        v
    "48656c6c6f"

BYTES:
    b"Hello"
        |
        | .decode()
        v
    "Hello"


14. MOST IMPORTANT EXAM MEMORY TRICK

    STRING --(.encode())--> BYTES --(.hex())--> HEX STRING

    STRING <--(.decode())-- BYTES <--(bytes.fromhex())-- HEX STRING


Quick reference:

    .encode()       = String -> Bytes
    b"Hello"        = directly create Bytes
    .decode()       = Bytes -> String
    .hex()          = Bytes -> Hex String
    bytes.fromhex() = Hex String -> Bytes

    encrypt()       = usually returns Bytes
    decrypt()       = usually returns Bytes

Remember:
    .hex() is NOT encryption.
    input() always returns a STRING.
    Padding is done on BYTES.
    DES block size = 8 bytes.
    AES block size = 16 bytes.
